## ASSEMBLED SECTION



### <img src="https://flagcdn.com/cz.svg" width="32" alt="Czech Republic" style={{display: 'inline', verticalAlign: 'middle', position: 'relative', top: '-2px', marginRight: '8px', border: '1px solid #d1d5db', borderRadius: '2px'}} />Czech Republic

The Czech Republic's constitutional crisis has entered a phase of sustained institutional stress testing, with 90,000 protesters and formal no-confidence procedures demonstrating that democratic safeguards remain active despite governmental overreach. While structural Western integration (F-35, KHNP nuclear, EU dependency) continues to constrain radical departures, the government is signaling more transactional approaches on Ukraine and China while explicitly rejecting NATO spending pressure. The crisis validates both the severity of democratic pressure under populist governance and the resilience of Czech civil society and institutional response mechanisms.

**Key developments:**

- **Domestic:** Approximately 90,000 Czechs rallied in Prague and other cities to support President Pavel against government overreach, organized by Million Moments for Democracy. Protests explicitly opposed 'vulgarity, arrogance and blackmail' in response to the Macinka affair, with over 600,000 signing support petitions.
- **Diplomatic:** Parliamentary Speaker Tomio Okamura criticized a 30-meter Ukrainian flag display during Unity Day celebrations, calling it an 'unnecessary provocation' and confirming government plans for stricter Ukrainian residency conditions. Czech churches condemned Okamura's statements as 'misleading and dangerous.'
- **Diplomatic:** Czech Foreign Minister Macinka outlined Prague's thinking on China policy to US Ambassador Merrick, with the government operating on assumption of a three-pole global order (US, China, Russia) and considering pragmatic China engagement while remaining anchored in the American camp.
- **Security:** The coalition confirmed defense spending will remain at approximately 2% GDP rather than increasing toward NATO's new 3.5% target. Speaker Okamura stated current levels are 'sufficient,' prioritizing domestic spending on families and transport over defense increases.
- **Institutional:** Opposition parties submitted 81 signatures for a no-confidence vote scheduled for next Tuesday, responding to the Macinka blackmail scandal. Despite lacking the 101 votes needed to succeed, opposition argues democracy cannot remain silent about governmental overreach.

<Accordion title="Other Stories">
- **Mass rallies support President Pavel amid constitutional crisis with government** — Tens of thousands of Czechs rallied in Prague and other cities to support President Petr Pavel in his dispute with the government over ministerial appointments. The protests were organized in response to Pavel's refusal to appoint Filip Turek as environment minister and subsequent tensions with Foreign Minister Macinka. *([efe.com](https://reuters.com/world/czechs-rally-support-president-his-growing-rift-with-government-2026-02-01))*
- **President Pavel accuses Foreign Minister Macinka of extortion over blocked ministerial appointment** — President Petr Pavel accused Foreign Minister Petr Macinka of potential extortion after blocking the appointment of Filip Turek as environment minister. Pavel said Macinka sent unacceptable text messages to his adviser threatening political consequences. *([politico.eu](https://www.politico.eu/article/czech-president-foreign-minister-petr-pavel-blackmail-politics-eu/))*
</Accordion>---

